The package QPALM 

  https://github.com/kul-optec/QPALM

is not included as part of GALAHAD, but may be
called optionally from GALAHAD's ubersolver QP package.

GALAHAD relies on two environment variables,
and if QPALM has not been installed, these 
are set by default as

  QPALMINCLUDE = $(GALAHAD)/src/dum/include
  QPALM = -lgalahad_qpalm

This allows GALAHAD to function, but any attempt
to use QPALM will result in an error return code.

If you wish to use QPALM ...

1. Download QPALM via git to a suitable location 

     git clone https://github.com/kul-optec/QPALM

   and thene move into the QPALM directory using

     cd ./QPALM

   we shall refer to this through the shell variable $QPALM

2. Install the package into a suitable subdirectory
   (here ./build/pc64.lnx.gfo for a gfortran build) using Cmake

     cmake -B build/pc64.lnx.gfo -S QPALM \
      -D CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Release \
      -D QPALM_WITH_CXX=On \
      -D QPALM_WITH_FORTRAN=On \
      -D QPALM_FORTRAN_64BIT_INDICES=Off \
      -D LADEL_64BIT_INDICES=Off \
      -D CMAKE_POSITION_INDEPENDENT_CODE=On
     cmake --build build/pc64.lnx.gfo --config Release -j

   You can use other compilers by, e.g., resetting them prior to
   the first call, e.g. (in the bash shell)

      CC=icx CXX=icpx FC=ifx cmake ....
   
   or by using the flags

     -D CMAKE_C_COMPILER=icx \
     -D CMAKE_CXX_COMPILER=icpx \ 
     -D CMAKE_Fortran_COMPILER=ifx \

   but remember to change the name of the build directory (to, e.g.,
   build/pc64.lnx.ifx) if you want to have two or more separate builds

3. Now call GALAHAD with the envirnment variables

      QPALM_BUILD = $(QPALM)/build/pc64.lnx.gfo
      QPALMINCLUDE = $(QPALM_BUILD)/interfaces/fortran/include
      QPALM = -lqpalm_fortran -lqpalm -lladel -lladel_amd \
        -L$(QPALM_BUILD) -L$(QPALM_BUILD)/interfaces/fortran \
        -L$(QPALM_BUILD)/LADEL -L$(QPALM_BUILD)/LADEL/SuiteSparse \
        -I$(QPALMINCLUDE)

